+    `bench/baselines/` passed the prefix test while the bytes landed outside the repo.
+    `../../outside.json`, `bench/baselines/../../evil.json` and a tracked-file overwrite
+    (`package.json`) are rejected too. Three further holes closed after review, each reproduced
+    before fixing:
+    - An existing target must be a **regular file with `nlink == 1`**. Checking only
+      `isSymbolicLink()` let a **hard link** through every layer (`lstat` says regular file,
+      `realpath` resolves back inside `bench/baselines/`, `O_NOFOLLOW` opens happily) — measured
+      result was exit code 0, zero warnings, and a file outside the repo overwritten by the
+      baseline JSON. A **FIFO** was worse: it passed the same gate and then `open(O_WRONLY)`
+      blocked forever with no reader, so the process hung after a full collection run with no
+      error output and no exit code at all.
+    - Writes go through a **same-directory temp file + `rename()`**, not a direct write.
+      `rename` swaps a directory entry: it does not follow symlinks, does not write into an
+      inode shared by a hard link, and cannot block on a FIFO. The temp file must be on the same
+      filesystem as the target (hence inside `bench/baselines/`, not `os.tmpdir()`), is created
+      `O_EXCL`, is `fsync`ed before the rename, and is removed on every failure path.
+    - Containment is re-checked **immediately before the write**, not only at startup: the
+      startup check exists for fast failure on bad configs, but collection runs for minutes, so
+      that check is long stale by the time bytes are written. Directory targets (`EISDIR`) and
+      read-only parents (`EACCES`) — the two most common write failures — are now rejected before
+      the dev server starts instead of after the whole run, which is what the startup check
+      claimed to be for. Existing baselines are **not** overwritten without `--force`, so two
+      collectors racing on the same `--output` can no longer have the later one silently win.
